-- title:
--   Witt coordinates on a closed subgroup come from the ambient group
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a perfect field of characteristic $p$ ($p$ prime), let $n$ be a nonzero natural number, let $A$ be a commutative ring carrying a $k$-Hopf algebra structure which is finite as a $k$-module and whose comultiplication is cocommutative, let $B$ be a commutative ring carrying a $k$-bialgebra structure, and let $\pi : A \to B$ be a surjective homomorphism of $k$-bialgebras. Here, for a $k$-bialgebra $C$, $\mathrm{wittHom}\ k\ p\ m\ C$ denotes the additive subgroup of truncated Witt vectors $x \in W_m(C)$ satisfying the additivity identity $W_m(\Delta)(x) = W_m(\iota_1)(x) + W_m(\iota_2)(x)$ in $W_m(C \otimes_k C)$, where $\Delta$ is the comultiplication and $\iota_1, \iota_2 : C \to C \otimes_k C$ the two inclusions; and $\mathrm{wittHomShift}\ k\ p\ m\ C$ is the additive map from level $m$ to level $m+1$ induced by Verschiebung on truncated Witt vectors. Assume that $\mathrm{wittHomShift}\ k\ p\ n\ A$ is surjective onto $\mathrm{wittHom}\ k\ p\ (n+1)\ A$. Then for every $y \in \mathrm{wittHom}\ k\ p\ n\ B$ and every index $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\ n$, the $i$-th Witt coordinate of $y$ lies in the image under $\pi$ of the $k$-subalgebra of $A$ generated by all Witt coordinates, at all indices $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\ n$, of all elements of $\mathrm{wittHom}\ k\ p\ n\ A$.
--
--   In the language of group schemes: if $G = \operatorname{Spec} A$ is a finite commutative group scheme over a perfect field of characteristic $p$ and $H = \operatorname{Spec} B$ is a closed subgroup, then at a level $n$ which saturates $G$ every homomorphism $H \to W_n$ has its coordinates in the image of the subalgebra of $A$ generated by the coordinates of the homomorphisms $G \to W_n$. It is the computational heart of the surjectivity statement [`HopfAlgebra.wittHomMap_surjective_of_surjective_of_wittHomShift_surjective`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.wittHomMap_surjective_of_surjective_of_wittHomShift_surjective), within the Dieudonné-theoretic analysis of finite flat group schemes used in the deformation-theoretic part of the argument.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_HopfAlgebra_wittHom_coeff_mem_map_adjoin_of_surjective_of_wittHomShift_surjective.lean

